    "lawCode": "Amd Arts IV, VII & XV-A, add Arts VII-B & VII-C, §1 of Chap 154 of 1921; amd §73-a, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7721\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 154 of the laws of 1921,\nrelating to the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey and to amend\nthe public officers law, in relation to enacting the \"port authority\nof New York and New Jersey transparency and accountability act of\n2014\" and in relation to the functioning of the port authority as an\nopen, transparent and accountable interstate public authority\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey\nto adhere to accountability and transparency standards that were\nimposed on New York state public authorities in 2005 and 2009.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill sets forth the title of the Act: \"The Port\nAuthority of New York and New Jersey Transparency and Accountability\nAct of 2014\".\n\nSection two amends Article IV of chapter 154 relating to the\ncommissioners of the Port Authority to set forth specific duties and\nresponsibilities to be performed by the board of commissioners. The\nsection would also require commissioners to perform their fiduciary\nduties as board members; to exercise independent judgment and act in\nthe best interest of the authority, its mission and the public served\nby the Authority. Each commissioner is required to certify in writing\nacknowledging his or her duty of loyalty and care to the authority and\nits mission.\n\nSection three makes a conforming amendment relating to the new Article\nVII-B.\n\nSection four adds a new Article VII-B to chapter 154 to add the\nfollowing additional duties and responsibilities to those currently\nperformed by the board of commissioners, including:\n\n*Setting forth specific procedures for disposing of Port Authority\nproperty;\n\n*Describing the specific information that must be included in the\nannual report of the Port Authority;\n\n*Requiring the financial statements of the Port Authority to be\nsubject to certain auditing standards, and imposing duties and\nresponsibilities on the auditing firm retained by the Port Authority\nto perform the audits;\n\n*Establishing a whistleblower program;\n\n*Requiring Port Authority officers and employees to report suspected\nfraud, corruption, conflict of interest to the Inspector General;\n\n*Requiring New York commissioners, all officers and certain employees\nof the port authority to file annual financial disclosure statements;\n\n\n*Complying with certain rules relating to contacts with lobbyists; and\n\n*Requiring the Port Authority to conduct a needs assessment and public\nhearings before raising tolls and fares.\n\nSection five amends Article XV-A of chapter 154 to specify that all\nmeetings of the Port Authority are public meetings subject to the open\nmeetings laws of both states, sets forth the process for declaring an\nexecutive session of the board, and require minutes to be taken in all\nmeetings of Port Authority.\n\nSection six amends the public officers law to require the New York\ncommissioners, and certain employees of the Port Authority to file\nannual financial disclosure statements.\n\nSection seven sets forth the severability clause.\n\nSection eight provides that the act shall take effect upon the\nenactment by the state of New Jersey of legislation that has an\nidentical effect with this act.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2005 and 2009, the state of New York enacted comprehensive\nlegislation to improve the practices of, and increase oversight over,\nthe state's public authorities. As entities that are publicly-funded\nand are created to perform or provide an important public service, it\nwas important to increase and enhance the accountability and\ntransparency of the public authorities.\n\nThe Public Authority of New York and New Jersey is a multi-billion\ndollar company that performs and provides vital transportation and\ninfrastructure functions and services in the state of New York. The\nPort Authority's operations include Newark, LaGuardia and Kennedy\nairports; the Port of New York and New Jersey, which is the leading\nmaritime cargo port on the east coast of North America; PATH mass\ntransit system; the World Trade Center; and numerous bridges and\ntunnels, including the George Washington Bridge and the Lincoln and\nHolland Tunnels.\n\nWith such a substantial and vital presence in this state, the Port\nAuthority should abide by and be subject to the same laws as are\ncurrently imposed on all other New York state public authorities. The\nmeasures contained in this legislation will bring accountability and\ntransparency to the Port Authority. It will also allow the public and\ngovernment officials to have confidence that the Authority is\ncommitted to its mission in a responsible and fiscally sound manner.\n\nIt is in the best interest of the public that the states of New York\nand New Jersey enact substantially identical legislation to ensure\nthat the Port Authority functions as an open, transparent and\naccountable interstate public authority.\n\nThis Act will not impair or otherwise affect the authority of the\nstate comptroller of both states under current law (Chap. 649 of the\nlaws of 1929; Uncons. Law, § 7071) to examine the accounts and books\nof the Port Authority.\n\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.3944, passed Assembly - 2013;\nA.9150-A, passed Assembly 2012.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect upon the enactment into law by the state of\nNew Jersey of legislation having an identical effect with this Act.\n\n",
